    {
      "id": "7ac08d20040a1c90cb3ea66cd97448d8140df617",
      "tag": "autogenerated:ai-review",
      "author": {
        "_account_id": 1345,
        "name": "Gerrit AI review for Lustre",
        "display_name": "AI review",
        "email": "gaireview@linuxhacker.ru",
        "username": "aireview",
        "status": "Contact Oleg Drokin (green@whamcloud.com) with any questions on this account"
      },
      "date": "2026-10-14 09:36:56.000000000",
      "message": "Patch Set 1:\n\n(1 comment)\n\nAI code review results below, please doublecheck\n\nLooks good; the sizing fix is correct (highest index written is LNET_DBG_RECOV_ATTR_MAX + lgdrl_count - 1 == count, so count + 1 entries are needed). The only request is the Fixes: line inline, which can ride along on any refresh.\n\nNot part of this patch, but lustre_stats_dump() in lustre/obdclass/kernelcomm.c looks like it has the same problem, possibly worse: it allocates LUSTRE_STATS_ATTR_MAX + ls_num - 1 entries but sets lkl_maxattr = LUSTRE_STATS_ATTR_MAX + ls_num and fills lkl_list[] up to that index, so both the fill loop and lnet_genl_parse_list() look like they run two entries past the allocation. Is it worth a follow-up under this ticket?",
      "accounts_in_message": [],
      "_revision_number": 1
    }
  ],
